Answer: theia is a technique/tool for localizing structural bugs in deep learning programs—such as issues with activation functions, layer/model properties, loss functions, data preprocessing, or missing/redundant/wrong layers—and recommending fixes. it uses dataset characteristics and parsed source code, supports fcnns and cnns for regression and classification, and works with keras and pytorch by detecting bugs early in training.

neuralint is a prior/state-of-the-art dl bug-detection approach used as a comparison baseline. theia detected 57/75 bugs in 40 real-world buggy dl programs, while neuralint detected 17/75.
